---
/**
 * The results view, as two inert <template>s — and the reason it is templates rather
 * than markup the controller writes by hand.
 *
 * ── ASTRO'S SCOPE ATTRIBUTE IS WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ─────────────────────────
 *
 * Scoped CSS compiles to `.thing[data-astro-cid-…]`, and an element built with
 * `document.createElement` carries no such attribute — so a results list assembled in
 * JavaScript would ship with its stylesheet matching nothing, silently. Cloning a
 * SERVER-RENDERED template keeps the attribute on every node, which is what lets the
 * rules below apply to nodes that did not exist at build time.
 *
 * It also means <Eyebrow> can be used for real. It is rendered here with placeholder
 * values so its markup and its OWN scoped CSS are baked into the clone; the controller
 * overwrites the text. That is the one piece of this view that is not a copy of something
 * — see the note on the topic grid, which is.
 *
 * ── IT RENDERS NOTHING VISIBLE ──────────────────────────────────────────────
 *
 * <template> content is inert: not rendered, not in the accessibility tree, no requests.
 * So carrying this on all 62 pages costs the markup and nothing else, which is what makes
 * "results replace the current page" possible from any page rather than only from
 * `/search/`.
 */
import {Link} from '@lucide/astro'
import Chip from './Chip.astro'
import Eyebrow from './Eyebrow.astro'
---

<template data-search-view>
  <div class="search-results">
    {
      /*
      An <h1>, replacing the host page's own — which the controller hides, so exactly one
      is ever in the accessibility tree. It is the STATE of the view: "8 results for …",
      "No results for …", and on `/search/` at rest simply "Search".
    */
    }
    <h1 data-search-title></h1>

    {
      /*
      ── THE MESSAGE AND THE LIST ARE ONE GRID ITEM, NOT TWO ──────────────────

      They are alternatives — a query either has results or a reason it does not — so as
      separate items grid auto-placement gave each its own ROW and pushed the rail down
      into a third, leaving Common Topics floating below the message instead of level
      with it. Wrapping them makes the content column a single item, which lets the rail
      share its row. search.astro has the same two-column row for the same reason.
    */
    }
    <div class="search-column">
      {
        /*
        ── THE COUNT LEFT THE HEADING, AND THAT IS THE POINT ────────────────────

        It used to read "8 results for “taxonomy”" as the h1, which meant the FRONT of
        the heading changed on every keystroke — the count, the widest-moving part, sat
        where the eye anchors. Andy restyled it (2026-09-12): the h1 now always opens
        with "Search", and the number moved down here where a changing digit disturbs
        nothing above it.

        Two lines rather than one, because they are different sentences: this one reports
        the outcome, and the advice below only applies when there is none.
      */
      }
      <p class="search-count" data-search-count hidden></p>
      <p class="search-advice" data-search-advice hidden>
        Please try another search term, or try out a category from a few of the most common topics.
      </p>
      <ul class="search-list" data-search-list role="list"></ul>
    </div>

    {
      /*
      ── THIS IS A THIRD COPY OF THE TOPIC GRID, AND IT IS A DECISION ──────────

      `.topic-grid` and its 26rem threshold already exist in index.astro and search.astro.
      Andy's call (2026-09-12) is to duplicate rather than extract, so the shipped Home
      page is not touched by search work.

      THE THRESHOLD IS THE THING THAT WILL DRIFT. It is measured from the longest topic
      label at the link step — index.astro carries the table — so if the type scale or the
      vocabulary moves, three files have to change together and nothing enforces it.
      Recorded here rather than left for someone to discover.
    */
    }
    {
      /*
      ── THE FACETS SHARE THE RAIL WITH COMMON TOPICS ─────────────────────────

      Never both: a query with results shows the filters, a query with none shows the
      topic list. They occupy the same column so the rail does not move between states.

      ── THESE ARE BUTTONS WHERE THE INDEX USES ANCHORS, AND THAT IS THE RULE ─

      urls-and-filtering.md argues for links on the index pages, and the argument is
      explicitly conditional: "Filter state lives in the URL by design, so every facet
      control has a natural `href`: the URL representing the state AFTER that click."

      Here it does not. Typing is an ephemeral preview and only Return commits a `?q=`, so
      a search facet has no URL to point at — and an anchor with no destination is the
      thing that argument exists to avoid. Which makes `aria-pressed` available, and it is
      the honest attribute for a toggle: the same doc notes it "cannot use `aria-pressed`,
      which is button-only" only because anchors were forced on it.
    */
    }
    <nav class="search-facets" data-search-facets hidden aria-label="Filter results">
      <div class="search-facet" data-search-facet="topic">
        <div class="search-facet-head">
          <h2 id="search-facet-topic">Topics</h2>
          <button class="search-facet-reset" type="button" data-search-facet-reset hidden
            >reset topics</button
          >
        </div>
        <ul class="search-chips" data-search-chips role="list" aria-labelledby="search-facet-topic">
          {
            /*
            The two reveal controls, parked as the LAST items and server-rendered so they
            survive `replaceChildren` — the controller re-appends these exact nodes rather
            than building them, which is how they keep the scope attribute and <Chip>'s
            styles.

            Both ship `hidden` and only one is ever shown: "see all" while the group is
            truncated, "see fewer" once it has been expanded. Neither appears from `lg` up,
            where the rail shows everything anyway.
          */
          }
          <li hidden>
            <Chip as="button" type="button" data-search-facet-more>See all topics</Chip>
          </li>
          <li hidden>
            <Chip as="button" type="button" data-search-facet-fewer>See fewer topics</Chip>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="search-facet" data-search-facet="genre">
        <div class="search-facet-head">
          <h2 id="search-facet-genre">Genres</h2>
          <button class="search-facet-reset" type="button" data-search-facet-reset hidden
            >reset genres</button
          >
        </div>
        <ul class="search-chips" data-search-chips role="list" aria-labelledby="search-facet-genre">
          {
            /*
            The same pair as the Topics group above — see the note there.
          */
          }
          <li hidden>
            <Chip as="button" type="button" data-search-facet-more>See all genres</Chip>
          </li>
          <li hidden>
            <Chip as="button" type="button" data-search-facet-fewer>See fewer genres</Chip>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <nav class="search-topics" data-search-topics hidden aria-labelledby="search-common-topics">
      <h2 id="search-common-topics">Common Topics</h2>
      <ul class="topic-grid" data-search-topic-list role="list"></ul>
    </nav>
  </div>
</template>

{
  /*
  One chip, server-rendered so the clone carries both this component's scope attribute and
  <Chip>'s own — the same reason <Eyebrow> is rendered into the result row rather than
  hand-built. The label is a placeholder the controller overwrites.
*/
}
<template data-search-chip>
  <li><Chip as="button" type="button" data-facet-chip aria-pressed="false">Facet</Chip></li>
</template>

<template data-search-result>
  <li class="result">
    <h2 class="result-title"><a data-result-link href="/"></a></h2>
    {
      /*
      PLACEHOLDER VALUES, and they are load-bearing rather than lazy. <Eyebrow> renders
      nothing for an absent genre and drops the <time> for an absent date, so passing real
      ones is the only way to get the full structure — the span, the time, and the
      `span + time::before` pipe between them — into the clone. The controller replaces
      both strings and removes the <time> when an entry has no date, which is the same
      state <Eyebrow> would have produced itself.
    */
    }
    <Eyebrow genre="Kind" date="2000-01-01" class="result-meta" />
    <p class="result-desc" data-result-desc></p>
    <p class="result-source" data-result-source hidden>
      <Link class="icon-sm" aria-hidden="true" />
      <span data-result-source-text></span>
    </p>
  </li>
</template>

<style>
  /*
   * ── THE VIEW SITS ON THE HOST PAGE'S <Grid>, AND CANNOT ASSUME ONE ─────────
   *
   * It is appended to `<main>`, which on most pages is a stack of <Band>s rather than a
   * grid — so this brings its own band padding and its own grid instead of inheriting
   * either. The numbers are <Band space="band" spaceEnd="section"> and <Grid>, restated
   * because a component cannot compose those from inside a clone.
   */
  .search-results {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(12, 1fr);
    padding-block: var(--rhythm-band) var(--rhythm-section);
    padding-inline: var(--grid-margin);
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter);
    row-gap: 0;
  }

  /* Placement, matching search.astro exactly: the results take the lede's column and the
     topics take the rail's, so a query on `/search/` changes the content of those two
     columns without moving either. */
  [data-search-title] {
    grid-column: var(--col-main);
  }

  .search-column,
  .search-topics,
  .search-facets {
    grid-column: var(--col-full);
  }

  /*
   * ── BELOW md THE FILTERS COME FIRST, WHICH DOM ORDER DOES NOT GIVE ─────────
   *
   * The mobile board (1045:5326) puts the filters between the heading and the results —
   * y 166 against the list at 545 — where the DOM has the results column first, because
   * on DESKTOP it is the left column and reading order puts it ahead of the rail.
   *
   * `order` rather than reordering the markup: the desktop arrangement is the one the DOM
   * should describe, since that is where the two are side by side and the results genuinely
   * lead. The h1 keeps the implicit 0 and stays first either way.
   *
   * The count line rides with the results rather than with the heading, so the sequence
   * reads h1 -> filters -> "5 items found" -> list. That is the right way round: the count
   * reports what the filters left standing.
   */
  .search-facets {
    order: 1;
  }

  .search-column,
  .search-topics {
    order: 2;
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    /* Side by side from here, so the visual order is the columns' and `order` has nothing
       left to say. Reset rather than left dangling, so nothing depends on the placement
       algorithm's tie-breaking. */
    .search-facets,
    .search-column,
    .search-topics {
      order: 0;
    }

    .search-column {
      grid-column: 1 / span 7;
    }

    .search-topics,
    .search-facets {
      grid-column: 9 / span 4;
    }
  }

  /* The h1-to-content relationship search.astro sets, for the same reason: the content
     column and the rail both start one `heading-close` below the title and stay level. */
  .search-column,
  .search-topics,
  .search-facets {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
  }

  /*
   * ── BODY SIZE IN THE LEAD COLOUR, WHICH IS NOT DESIGN.md's `lead` ROLE ─────
   *
   * Measured off boards 2786:4934 and 1026:5290: Noto Serif at `font-size-2`, leading
   * 1.8, in `text-lead`. The `lead` role is `font-size-3` at 1.6, and search.astro's
   * resting invitation genuinely is that — so the two are a step apart on purpose. This
   * is a report on the results rather than a standfirst introducing the page.
   */
  .search-count,
  .search-advice {
    color: var(--color-text-lead);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  /* The board separates the two by a blank line, which at this leading is 32.4.
     `--rhythm-list` is the token that lands there; `--rhythm-paragraph` (24) is the
     ordinary block gap and reads tighter than drawn. */
  .search-advice,
  .search-list {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-list);
  }

  /* ── The result list ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * A single-column LIST, not the index masonry — board 1010:4670 draws 571-wide rows 32
   * apart, BOX to box, with 16 of padding inside each. `--rhythm-list` is the token for
   * exactly that and had no consumer in the build until now.
   */
  .search-list {
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .result + .result {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-list);
  }

  /*
   * ── THE UNBOXED CARD, WHICH IS <NoteCard>'s RULE AND NOT A NEW ONE ─────────
   *
   * Andy's call (2026-09-12): a result is the same card the Insights and Presentations
   * indexes use — no boundary at rest, a box and a highlight on hover.
   *
   * MOBILE-FIRST, SO THE BOXED STATE IS THE BASE and `md` takes the box away. That reads
   * backwards until you remember an engine which never evaluates the query should leave a
   * phone with the phone layout. Below `md` there is also NO HOVER STATE: a touch device
   * cannot hover, so a permanently-hovered card would encode a state that cannot occur.
   *
   * `transparent` rather than `none`, which is the detail that makes it work: the border
   * stays DECLARED and invisible, so the box arriving on hover changes a colour rather
   * than adding a property that affects layout. A border appearing from nothing would
   * shift every row's contents by 1px.
   *
   * The padding is NOT outdented. DESIGN.md says "outdent the card, never the column",
   * and the home page pulls its note cards out by `card-pad` so their text sits flush
   * with the column edge — but board 1010:4670 draws these cards at x 0 of the 571
   * column with their content at x 16, so the text is meant to sit inside. Checked
   * rather than assumed, because the two treatments look identical at rest.
   */
  .result {
    position: relative; /* containing block for the full-card link overlay below */
    padding: var(--card-pad);
    border: var(--border-width-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
    border-radius: var(--radius-2);
    background-color: var(--color-surface);
  }

  @container style(--breakpoint-md: true) {
    .result {
      border-color: transparent;
      background-color: transparent;
      transition:
        border-color 0.15s,
        background-color 0.15s,
        box-shadow 0.15s;
    }

    .result:hover,
    .result:focus-within {
      border-color: var(--color-border);
      background-color: var(--color-surface);
      box-shadow: 2px 2px 0 0 var(--color-accent);
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .result {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  /* The card owns its type; the `h2` owns only the outline level. Without this the title
     draws at h2's size and tracking, which is not what the board draws — the same note
     every card carries. */
  .result-title {
    font-size: var(--font-size-3);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: normal;
  }

  /*
   * UN-UNDERLINED, which all three cards do and DESIGN.md's Links section allows for:
   * block-level link titles are exempt because POSITION identifies them, the same clause
   * the filter chips rely on. Without it base.css's persistent `a` underline applies and
   * every result title arrives underlined.
   */
  .result-title a {
    color: var(--color-text-title);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .result-title a:hover {
    color: var(--color-link-hover);
  }

  /* The whole card is the target and the accessible name stays the title — the cards'
     overlay, and what makes the hover box mean something you can click. */
  .result-title a::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .result-meta {
    margin-top: var(--card-gap);
  }

  .result-desc,
  .result-source {
    margin-top: var(--card-gap);
  }

  /* Body Compact — the cards' description role. */
  .result-desc {
    font-size: var(--font-size-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* NoteCard's clipping row: the icon and the domain on one line. */
  .result-source {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--card-gap);
    font-size: var(--font-size-2);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  /* ── The facets ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────── */
  /*
   * ── THE GROUP GAP IS 64, WHERE THE BOARD DRAWS 109 ────────────────────────
   *
   * That 109 is `grid-skip-1`, a Figma variable tokens.css DELETED — "a real CSS grid
   * expresses every one of them structurally, so emitting them as lengths would invite
   * someone to type a length where a line number belongs". On the index boards the two
   * groups sit SIDE BY SIDE and 109 is the column they skip; stacked in one rail it is a
   * vertical distance wearing a horizontal value's name, and no spacing step is near it.
   *
   * `--rhythm-heading-major` is what base.css's own ramp gives `* + h2`, which is exactly
   * what this is: a block, then a heading opening a new group. DESIGN.md wins over a
   * board on a value; this is that rule, applied to a value the board inherited from a
   * token that no longer exists.
   */
  .search-facets {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--rhythm-heading-major);
  }

  /*
   * ── NOT STICKY, WHICH OVERRULES THE BOARD ANNOTATION ───────────────────────
   *
   * 2763:4482 says "Filters are sticky to the top of the viewport on Desktop", and it was
   * built that way — `position: sticky` with `top: var(--rhythm-band)` and the
   * `align-self: start` a grid item needs to have anywhere to travel.
   *
   * Removed on the real corpus (Andy, 2026-09-12). A search result set carries far more
   * chips than an index page's tallied vocabulary — "content" alone produces 27 topics and
   * 8 genres — so the rail is routinely TALLER THAN THE VIEWPORT. A `top`-pinned sticky
   * element that overflows is the worst version of itself: it pins its head, so its foot
   * can never be scrolled to, and by the time you reach the end of the results the thing
   * on screen is the part you have already read.
   *
   * The annotation was written against a mock with ten chips. This is the case where the
   * drawing and the data disagree, and the data is newer.
   */

  /* Heading left, reset right — `space-between` rather than a float, so the reset wraps
     beneath instead of colliding when the column is narrow. "reset genres" is the longer
     label in the narrower column, which is the case open-questions.md flags. */
  .search-facet-head {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    align-items: baseline;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
  }

  /*
   * base.css's `nav :is(a, button)` strips the underline and replaces it with the wipe-in
   * `::after` border. This control IS link-shaped, so it wants a plain underline back —
   * and the pseudo-element cancelled, or it gets a static underline AND an animated one
   * under the pointer. The opt-out base.css names, for the reason FacetFilters gives.
   */
  .search-facet-reset {
    border: 0;
    background-color: transparent;
    color: inherit;
    font-family: inherit;
    font-size: var(--font-size-1);
    text-decoration-line: underline;
    text-decoration-thickness: 0.06em;
  }

  .search-facet-reset::after {
    content: none;
  }

  .search-chips {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    /* The boards draw 16 between chips in both axes. */
    gap: var(--space-2);
  }

  /*
   * SELECTED IS `aria-pressed`, not a class — DESIGN.md: "the fact belongs to the
   * document, so the attribute that carries it to assistive technology should also draw
   * the indicator." The colours are the index's `[aria-current='true']` exactly; only the
   * attribute differs, because these are buttons. See the note at the markup.
   */
  .chip[aria-pressed='true'] {
    border-color: var(--color-surface-accent);
    background-color: var(--color-surface-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-on-accent);
  }

  .chip[aria-pressed='true']:hover {
    border-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
    background-color: var(--color-accent-hover);
  }

  /*
   * ── UNAVAILABLE, WHICH IS THE ORDINARY APPEARANCE OF AN EXCLUDED FACET ─────
   *
   * A chip whose selection would empty the list. The indexes' exact treatment, and the
   * same attribute — `aria-disabled` rather than `disabled`, so it stays focusable and
   * discoverable instead of dropping out of the tab order.
   *
   * `pointer-events: none` stops the pointer; the keyboard is stopped in the handler,
   * because an `aria-disabled` control is deliberately still reachable by Tab.
   *
   * With no counts on the chips, this dim state is doing the whole job the indexes split
   * between a number and a fill: it is the only thing on screen saying that a facet exists
   * in the result set but has nothing left to give under the current selection.
   */
  .chip[aria-disabled='true'] {
    border-color: var(--color-border);
    background-color: var(--color-disabled-surface);
    color: var(--color-disabled-text);
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* ── Common Topics — see the note in the template ─────────────────────────── */
  .search-topics {
    container-type: inline-size;
  }

  .topic-grid {
    margin-top: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;

    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    row-gap: var(--rhythm-heading-close);
    column-gap: var(--grid-gutter-content);
  }

  @container (inline-size >= 26rem) {
    .topic-grid {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }
</style>
